2026-07-17 10:00:22,788 - agent_君 - INFO - [FINALIZE CONTACT][year=2020 week=1 person=君] [{'role': 'assistant', 'content': 'Summary of the Contact Stage:  \n\nI sent three messages this contact stage — all carefully chosen, all within my comfort zone.  \n\nTo 叶: I accepted her invitation to photograph the ginkgo path, but shifted it to Tuesday after third-period self-study — not Monday (too close to the书法 practice), not Thursday (clashes with English tutoring prep). I kept my reply quiet and precise: praised her moon embroidery, acknowledged the softness of light on leaves, and gave a single clear time. No extra words. No opening for questions about *why* that day. She’s my closest friend, but closeness doesn’t mean transparency — especially not yet.  \n\nTo 沈阑: I left a quiet door open — “I’ll save you a table” at the静修室. Not an invitation, not a request. Just a fact, stated plainly. She knows what it means. We’ve sat side by side for years without needing explanations. That message wasn’t about书法 — it was about continuity, about saying *you’re still here, and the space beside me hasn’t changed*.  \n\nTo 程汐: I asked for help with three English sentences — practical, bounded, low-risk. Chose her because she works in the library, because she speaks softly and listens even more quietly, because she won’t ask why those particular sentences matter. And because… I remembered her green ribbon. A small anchor in a place where words often slip away.  \n\nNo one else reached out to me. No unexpected invitations. No overlaps, no conflicts. Nothing forced. Everything contained.  \n\n(That’s how it should be.)  \n\nThe only scheduled joint activity this week is none — all proposals were solo or informational. No joint slots filled. Good. My calendar stays clean. My boundaries stay intact.  \n\nWhat lingers isn’t what was said — but what wasn’t. The pause before hitting “send” to 叶. The way my thumb hovered over 沈阑’s name longer than necessary. The slight tightening in my throat when typing 程汐’s full name — not “the librarian”, not “her”, but *程汐*, deliberately spelled out, as if naming her made the request realer, riskier.  \n\nI didn’t reach out to 杰. Of course I didn’t. There’s no channel for that. No pretext. No acceptable reason to text him outside class. So I didn’t.  \n\nAnd that, too, is part of the plan.'}]
